, she saw combat service during the First World War and served as an administrative centre in the Second World War.","wpu":"HMS Caroline (1914)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/HMS_Caroline.jpg/960px-HMS_Caroline.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Samson","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":54.609595,"lo":-5.897942,"d":1164,"wp":"en:Samson and Goliath (cranes)","ds":"Samson and Goliath are the twin shipbuilding gantry cranes at Queen's Island, Belfast, Northern Ireland. The cranes, which were named after the Biblical figures Samson and Goliath, dominate the Belfast skyline and are landmark structures.","wpu":"Samson and Goliath (cranes)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Samson_and_Goliath_2018-07-27.jpg/960px-Samson_and_Goliath_2018-07-27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Olympic Slipway","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":54.609617,"lo":-5.908685,"d":1282},{"n":"Titanic Slipway","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":54.609733,"lo":-5.909216,"d":1286},{"n":"Titanic Belfast","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":54.608125,"lo":-5.909875,"d":1465,"w":"https://www.titanicbelfast.com/","ph":"+44 28 9076 6386","wp":"en:Titanic Belfast","ds":"Titanic Belfast is a visitor attraction in Belfast, Northern Ireland, which opened in 2012. It is a monument to Belfast's maritime heritage on the site of the former Harland & Wolff shipyard in the city's Titanic Quarter where the RMS Titanic was built. It tells the stories of the Titanic, which hit an iceberg and sank during her maiden voyage in 1912, and her sister ships RMS Olympic and HMHS Britannic.","wpu":"Titanic Belfast","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Titanic_Belfast_HDR.jpg/960px-Titanic_Belfast_HDR.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Titanic Hotel","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":54.607784,"lo":-5.908822,"d":1472,"w":"https://www.titanichotelbelfast.com/#hp1","ph":"+44 28 9508 2000","wifi":1},{"n":"SS Nomadic","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":54.606356,"lo":-5.911149,"d":1678,"w":"https://www.nomadicbelfast.com/","wp":"en:SS Nomadic (1911)","ds":"SS Nomadic is a former tender of the White Star Line, launched on 25 April 1911 at Belfast, that is now on display in Belfast's Titanic Quarter. She was built to transfer passengers and mail to and from the ocean liners RMS Olympic and RMS Titanic. She is the only surviving vessel designed by Thomas Andrews, who also helped design those two ocean liners, and the last White Star Line vessel in existence today.","wpu":"SS Nomadic","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/IMO_5516111_SS_Nomadic%2C_1911_20180914_113426_%2846770595105%29.jpg/960px-IMO_5516111_SS_Nomadic%2C_1911_20180914_113426_%2846770595105%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Goliath","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":54.604729,"lo":-5.905015,"d":1728,"wp":"en:Samson and Goliath (cranes)","ds":"Samson and Goliath are the twin shipbuilding gantry cranes at Queen's Island, Belfast, Northern Ireland. The cranes, which were named after the Biblical figures Samson and Goliath, dominate the Belfast skyline and are landmark structures.","wpu":"Samson and Goliath (cranes)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Samson_and_Goliath_2018-07-27.jpg/960px-Samson_and_Goliath_2018-07-27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Port Mark","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":54.604461,"lo":-5.913046,"d":1921},{"n":"Junction Mark","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":54.604461,"lo":-5.913165,"d":1924},{"n":"Starboard Mark","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":54.604397,"lo":-5.913237,"d":1933},{"n":"Premier Inn","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":54.604014,"lo":-5.912702,"d":1956,"w":"https://www.premierinn.com/gb/en/hotels/northern-ireland/antrim/belfast/belf